// @no-instantiate
// @hint redundant type annotation: `f` is inferred as `i32` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `q` is inferred as `{b: string, a: i32}` — remove it to lean on inference
// XFAIL-MISCOMPILE (the kind's first member; see README.vl). This program is WELL-TYPED and
// the checker is RIGHT to accept it — the 2026-08 ruling is that `{a: i32, b: string}` and
// `{b: string, a: i32}` are THE SAME TYPE, field order carrying no meaning for an ordinary
// object type. `assignable` agrees and takes `Q` where `P` is asked for.
//
// The EMITTER does not. `shapeRowSetId` walks a row's fields POSITIONALLY (`sFieldStart[si]
// + i`), so two field-permuted twins hash to different ids, intern as different rows, and
// emit two distinct wasm struct types:
//
//   (type 0 (struct (field (mut i32))     (field (mut (ref 2)))))   ;; P
//   (type 1 (struct (field (mut (ref 2))) (field (mut i32))))       ;; Q
//
// `vl check --codegen` exits 0 and `vl run` reports
// `type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)`. Pre-existing on master.
//
// THE FIX IS A LANGUAGE-DESIGN CALL, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS PINNED RATHER THAN FIXED. Two
// readings of the ruling, with different costs:
//   (a) CANONICAL LAYOUT — sort a row's fields at intern time so the twins become one row.
//       Assignment is then free, but field order changes globally: every `struct.get` index,
//       every literal construction order, every field table. And the FLAT-type exception
//       ("flat types do guarantee structural ordering") must be carved out, since their
//       offsets are their layout.
//   (b) COERCION AT THE BOUNDARY — keep both reps and emit a field-reordering copy where one
//       flows into the other. Local change, but a per-assignment runtime cost, and it makes
//       two "identical" types observably different in performance.
//
// The ruling settles that they are the SAME TYPE; it does not settle which of these the
// emitter should do about it.
// THE SECOND HINT IS THE BUG SAYING ITS OWN NAME: the checker reports `q` as inferred
// `{b: string, a: i32}` — the PERMUTED spelling — and still accepts it where `P` is asked
// for, because to the checker they are one type. The emitter reads the same two spellings
// and mints two rows.
type P = { a: i32, b: string }

type Q = { b: string, a: i32 }

function f(x: P): i32 {
  x.a
}

const q: Q = { b: "s", a: 7 }

print(f(q))
